\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Convention}
\title{Trace quality, supervision horizon, and productive persistence}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}
\tag{state}
\tag{harness}

\p{A long-horizon training trace should be selected for the behavior it
preserves, the evidence it exposes, and the cost it consumes. Let a trace be}
##{
  T=(o_{0:H},a_{0:H},y_{0:H},e_{0:H},v_{0:H},\tau,\sigma),
}
\p{where #{o} are observations, #{a} actions, #{y} outcomes, #{e} evidence
and environment-state references, #{v} verification or recovery events,
#{\tau} is the termination event, and #{\sigma} records the state boundary
under which the trace was produced. Token length is one coordinate of #{T}; it
does not replace decision horizon, verification, recovery, or state context.}

\p{The direct observation in
\citet{Abstract; Sections 3-5 and Tables 1-4}{dang2026selecting} compares
4K, 8K, 12K, and 16K supervision horizons for terminal-agent SFT. In its
reported Terminal-Bench v1.0 setting, 12K reaches more tasks than 16K with
about thirty percent less training time. The shorter setting under-persisted,
the longest setting over-persisted, and the intermediate setting produced the
best reported balance. This is a finite terminal-agent observation under one
model, teacher corpus, scaffold, and benchmark family; it does not establish
a universal horizon, long-term memory, or persistent identity.}

\p{The paper's bias-complexity account gives the FTIP hypothesis. Truncation
can increase temporal-supervision bias by removing later decisions, while a
longer continuation increases the policy complexity and finite-sample burden.
The useful horizon is therefore conditional on the task law, teacher policy,
model capacity, verifier, and retained state. A horizon comparison must keep
those coordinates visible rather than treating context length as the cause.}

\p{Selective refinement makes the data intervention explicit. A warm-start
model is trained on a shorter horizon, continuation segments are scored by
teacher-forced likelihood, and a fixed fraction is retained for longer-horizon
refinement. The score measures compatibility with the warm-start policy, not
intrinsic truth or universal quality: high-likelihood selection can improve
repeatability, while low-likelihood or random selection can preserve breadth,
rare strategies, and recovery behavior. At a fixed retained budget, report
both selection and coverage; quantity remains relevant when it buys coverage
or variance reduction.}

\p{For FTIP, call a run \strong{premature} when it terminates before the
declared evidence or recovery condition, \strong{productive} when it continues
through bounded evidence gathering, verification, and recovery, and
\strong{over-persistent} when additional work repeats or explores after the
task-relevant value has saturated. Record coverage loss separately: a selector
can improve repeatability while deleting hard tasks or unusual recovery paths.
These terms describe observable behavior; they are not claims about an agent's
memory or inner objective.}

\p{The trace intervention belongs beside the lifecycle intervention in
\ref{ftip-00P0}. A fixed-budget comparison should cross horizon and selection
with the state masks that retain or clear session context, candidate history,
workspace, and the verified best candidate. At minimum compare full-long,
verification-preserving intermediate truncation, top-likelihood, bottom-
likelihood, and random selection at the same retained budget. Hold the model,
teacher, scaffold, task split, verifier, seed law, and total token and compute
budgets fixed; charge selection, storage, restart, and discarded-trace costs.}

\p{The primary finite record should report successful tasks and attempts,
repeatability, premature-stop, productive-persistence, over-persistence,
recovery, verification, task and strategy coverage, retained tokens, total
training compute, wall time, storage, and selection overhead. Pair every
selected trace with its unselected continuation and reserve held-out tasks and
a cross-benchmark split. These are FTIP protocol requirements, not results
already established by the source.}

\p{A scoped search of Forest, formal-math, TauCetiReview, and TauCetiWorker
found no declaration corresponding to this trace record or its
quality-by-quantity estimands. Existing state, reset, replay, and cost
conventions are reused without claiming a formalization. The resulting
boundary is: train on the shortest trace that preserves the target decision,
evidence, verification, and recovery events, and test whether selection has
removed coverage or merely removed repetition.}
